<SpikeArrest name="SA-SpikeArrest">
  <DisplayName>Spike Arrest</DisplayName>
  <!--
    100ps = 6,000 requests/minute. Raised 10ps -> 20ps -> 100ps on 2026-09-15,
    matching aihub-api-v1's rate.

    WHAT THIS POLICY IS FOR CHANGED, and that is the point of this note. The
    original 10ps was derived from a downstream quota: Model Armor's 1,200 QPM,
    divided by the two scans each gateway call cost, gave a hard ceiling of 600
    calls/minute, and SpikeArrest existed to trip BEFORE Model Armor did so the
    caller got a clean 429 instead of a hard scan failure.

    Model Armor was removed from this proxy on 2026-09-15
    (apigee/disabled/model-armor/), so there is no longer a downstream quota
    for this policy to protect. At 100ps it is no longer a capacity control at
    all: it is a runaway-client backstop, several times above any traffic
    this platform has been observed to produce.

    Measured production traffic for scale (aicoeprod logs, direct-to-Vertex):
      one ~90,000-char Translation document   236 calls in 158s
      2-3 concurrent large-document jobs      12-17 calls/sec in ~7s bursts
      busiest single minute observed          191 calls
      one Sales-Agent research job            ~34 calls over ~14.5 min
    So 100ps sits roughly 6x above the highest burst ever measured. Nothing
    legitimate should reach it; if it trips, treat that as a bug in a caller
    rather than as a limit to raise.

    THE REAL CEILING IS NOW VERTEX AI's own per-project/per-region quota, and
    it has NOT been checked against 6,000/min. This policy no longer stands
    between callers and that limit, so exceeding it surfaces as 429s from the
    TARGET rather than from here. That is the first place to look if throttling
    reappears, and the number to verify before assuming this proxy can pass
    6,000 calls/minute.

    ON RESTORING MODEL ARMOR: this MUST come back down. The relationship
        max gateway calls/min = Model Armor QPM / 2
    returns the moment the scan policies re-enter the flow, and 100ps against
    the default 1,200 QPM over-subscribes by 10x. These are one ceiling
    expressed in two places. See apigee/disabled/model-armor/README.md.

    UseEffectiveCount is mandatory, not stylistic: without it SpikeArrest
    counts per message processor, so the effective limit silently multiplies by
    the number of MPs. ci/deploy-apigee-config.sh gates on its presence.

    Note SpikeArrest SMOOTHS rather than bucketing: 100ps admits roughly one
    request per 10ms, it does not allow 100 at once and then pause.
  -->
  <Rate>100ps</Rate>
  <UseEffectiveCount>true</UseEffectiveCount>
</SpikeArrest>
